artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider strongly interacting matter produced in a high-energy nuclear collision, in which a deconfined phase of quarks and gluons (quark-gluon plasma) reconfines into hadrons either via a smooth crossover (at small baryon chemical potential) or via a possible phase transition (at larger baryon chemical potential). After confinement, the density of hadrons remains high for a finite time, and the in-medium spectral properties of hadrons differ from their vacuum properties. Short-lived mesons—most notably the chiral partners ρ(770) and a1(1260)—contribute to electromagnetic emission (real photons and dileptons) through hadronic interactions and decays, and their in-medium spectral functions encode information about chiral symmetry and its restoration across the hadron–quark-gluon transition.

Problem. Develop a quantitative framework that uses measured photon and dilepton emission spectra from such collisions to:
(a) determine the finite-temperature and finite-baryon-density spectral properties of hadrons in the hot/dense medium;
(b) extract the temperature- and density-dependent behavior of chiral partners, in particular the ρ and a1(1260) vector and axial-vector spectral functions, and their degeneracy as a signature of chiral symmetry restoration;
(c) characterize the transition between the hadronic phase and the deconfined phase of quarks and gluons through the evolution of these electromagnetic emissivities.

In particular, identify observables in the photon and dilepton spectra (invariant mass, transverse momentum, polarization, centrality and beam-energy dependence) that disentangle the contributions of short-lived hadrons after confinement from other emission sources, and connect them rigorously to the in-medium vector and axial-vector correlators predicted by QCD (e.g., via Weinberg-type sum rules and lattice QCD constraints).
